summary:
- topic: FY26 Budget Presentation
  text: The Superintendent presented the FY26 budget, emphasizing a requested $3.17
    million increase from taxpayers despite a significant decline in student enrollment
    over the past three years. The budget aims to preserve core programs while respecting
    taxpayers.
- topic: Student Enrollment and Staffing Trends
  text: Data showed a projected lowest student enrollment in over 15 years for FY26,
    alongside an increase of 88 staff members and a decrease of 197 students since
    2017. There was also a notable decline in multilingual and McKinney-Vento eligible
    students.
- topic: Taxpayer Burden and Property Valuations
  text: The Superintendent highlighted a 38% increase in school tax burden for the
    average homeowner due to rising property valuations, noting that 61% of local
    taxes fund the school department. This shift in tax burden from commercial to
    residential property was discussed as a contributing factor to declining enrollment.
- topic: Proposed Staff Reductions and Funding Shifts
  text: The budget proposes eliminating several positions, including the Director
    of Curriculum, HR Coordinator, a Community Partnerships position, two ESOL teachers,
    three SEL teachers, and a McKinney-Vento social worker. Conversely, 11.5 teaching
    positions in special education, literacy, math, and behavioral support are being
    saved by shifting their funding from federal to the local budget.
- topic: Elimination of Second Lunch Program
  text: The budget eliminates funding for the second lunch option, saving $150,000.
    The Superintendent expressed confidence in securing community partnerships to
    provide shelf-stable snacks as an alternative.
- topic: Community and Staff Concerns on Cuts
  text: Numerous community members, including teachers, parents, and students, voiced
    strong opposition to cuts in student-facing roles like SEL teachers, ESOL teachers,
    and the percussion ed tech. They argued these cuts contradict district priorities,
    negatively impact vulnerable students, and increase teacher workload.
- topic: Curriculum Leadership and Budget Transparency
  text: Concerns were raised about the elimination of the Director of Curriculum position,
    with many arguing it is vital for academic excellence and consistency across schools.
    Public speakers also questioned the transparency of the budget process and the
    lack of a permanent finance director.
- topic: Athletic Complex Funding Discussion
  text: The Superintendent clarified that the proposed athletic complex renovation,
    to be voted on in November, would not impact the FY26 budget. Board members and
    public speakers debated the appropriateness of pursuing such a project amidst
    significant staff cuts and taxpayer burden.
