    principles were applied to the FY26 budget.'
- time: 00:09:55
  seconds: 595
  topic: Key Budget Facts and Taxpayer Ask
  desc: The FY26 budget requests over $3 million in additional funds from taxpayers,
    bringing the total to be raised by taxes to over $56 million. The school operating
    budget increase is slightly below 6%.
- time: 00:11:00
  seconds: 660
  topic: Student Enrollment Decline
  desc: The Superintendent presented a chart showing a significant decline in student
    enrollment from 2022 to an estimated 2025, with a projected loss of 300 students
    (10%). He noted that next year is expected to have the lowest enrollment in over
    15 years.
- time: 00:12:55
  seconds: 775
  topic: Staff to Student Ratio and Demographic Shifts
  desc: The district has 88 more benefits-eligible staff members and 197 fewer students
    compared to 2017, with an additional 50-60 student decrease expected next year.
    There has also been a significant decline in multilingual and McKinney-Vento eligible
    students, impacting service needs.
- time: 00:18:43
  seconds: 1123
  topic: Property Tax Impact on Residents
  desc: The Superintendent detailed the substantial increase in property taxes for
    average single-family residential property owners, with a 38% increase (over $1,100)
    due to rising property valuations and a slight mill rate increase. He noted that
    61% of local taxes go to the school department, and the tax burden is shifting
    from commercial to residential properties.
- time: 00:27:50
  seconds: 1670
  topic: Budget Increases vs. Inflation
  desc: The Superintendent presented data showing that the district's ask of taxpayers
    has consistently been above the inflation rate in recent years. He acknowledged
    this is a 'hard sell' to city council and taxpayers, especially those on fixed
    incomes.
- time: 00:30:33
  seconds: 1833
  topic: Reliance on Federal and Carryover Funds
  desc: The district has budgeted to use $600,000 in federal carryover funds, which
    will be fully expended this year. Additionally, an over-budgeting of federal title
    funds in FY25 means a $100,000 adjustment is needed for FY26, creating pressure
    on the local budget.
- time: 00:32:13
  seconds: 1933
  topic: Positions Saved by Funding Shifts
  desc: 11.5 teaching positions, including behavioral strategists, resource room teachers,
    a speech language expert, and a self-contained classroom teacher, are being moved
    from federal/carryover funding to the local budget. This decision aims to save
    these valued positions in the district.
- time: 00:33:36
  seconds: 2016
  topic: Additional Positions Saved
  desc: Six other positions, totaling $775,000, are also being saved, including literacy
    and math specialists at Skillin and Kaler, a regular education behavior strategist,
    and a regular education social worker. These positions reflect the board's priority
    to support schools with high rates of economically disadvantaged students.
- time: 00:34:26
  seconds: 2066
  topic: Proposed Reductions in Force
  desc: The Superintendent detailed proposed reductions, including the Director of
    Curriculum, HR Coordinator, a part-time Community Partnerships position, a STEM
    Coordinator, an Instructional Strategist, and the high school's student engagement
    coordinator. Other cuts include two ESOL teachers, three SEL teachers, one McKinney-Vento
    social work position, a classroom teacher, a general music teacher, a special
    education position, and a technology integrator.
- time: 00:39:04
  seconds: 2344
  topic: Elimination of Second Lunch Option
  desc: The budget eliminates funding for the second lunch option, a program costing
    $150,000 annually. The Superintendent expressed confidence in finding community
    partners to provide shelf-stable snacks to address student nutritional needs.
- time: 00:40:15
  seconds: 2415
  topic: Education Foundation Initiative
  desc: The district plans to move forward with establishing an education foundation
    for South Portland, similar to successful models in neighboring communities. This
    initiative aims to secure additional resources for students and staff, becoming
    a new focus for community partnerships.
- time: 00:41:43
  seconds: 2503
  topic: Athletic Complex Impact on FY26 Budget
  desc: The Superintendent clarified that the proposed athletic complex renovation
